{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New owner's duty to obtain and return transferred security deposits","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit plus accrued interest from the former owner, and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving all notices, paying interest and returning the deposit with accrued earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential security deposits held by a landlord; the duties attach to the new owner when the property is sold or transferred, whether or not the deposit and interest were actually transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Civil penalty for willfully withholding deposits of tenants receiving government assistance","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds the security deposit of a tenant who received state or federal financial, welfare or rental assistance may be liable for a civil penalty of $500 to $2,000 for each offense.","key_value":"$500-$2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Security deposits made by or on behalf of tenants who have received financial assistance through a state or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, collected and enforced by summary proceeding under the Penalty Enforcement Law; penalty amounts recovered go to the state entity that made the deposits.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Criminal penalty for unlawful use of security deposit monies","requirement":"A person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be criminally charged as a disorderly person and may be fined at least $200, imprisoned for up to 30 days, or both.","key_value":"$200 fine or up to 30 days' imprisonment, or both","coverage_conditions":"Security deposit monies held by a landlord.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both, as a disorderly persons offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-25","quoted_span":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be c riminally charged as a disorderly person and may be subject to a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord must have good cause to evict or refuse to renew a residential lease, and may recover possession only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process. The cause, except nonpayment of rent, must be described in a written notice to quit.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Dwelling units used as a residence; the Act protects blameless tenants and applies whether the lease is written or oral.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act limits on rent increases","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; to increase rent the landlord must terminate the old lease, offer a new lease at the higher rent and serve valid notice. An increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with any municipal rent control ordinance.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Residential tenants under written or oral leases; increases must comply with municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances where they exist.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"New Jersey has no state rent control law; municipal rent control or rent leveling ordinances govern rent increases where adopted.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","key_value":"30 years following completion of construction","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the exemption period runs from completion of construction of the building.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances; HUD-subsidized or HUD-insured projects and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Municipalities may enact rent control ordinances; this state law exempts newly constructed multiple dwellings from them for 30 years.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Ban on rent increases to cover condominium conversion costs","requirement":"When a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion. Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are still allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in the converted building, whether or not they are eligible for protected tenancy as senior citizens or disabled persons.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Increases to cover the cost of new services or amenities are not prohibited.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
